diff --git a/docs/deep-idle-research.md b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
index 8a6476a25050..94538aa4d069 100644
--- a/docs/deep-idle-research.md
+++ b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
@@ -632,6 +632,18 @@ tables.
   No Linux code manages memory controller power states on Apple; if the
   PMP owns them, the PMP (E1) matters for s2idle as well as idle.
 
+* Whether an idle P cluster keeps its last clock and voltage. schedutil
+  re-evaluates a policy only on scheduler events of its CPUs, so a
+  cluster that goes idle keeps the last pstate it asked for; whether the
+  hardware lowers the voltage by itself once every core is down is not
+  known. `scaling_cur_freq` reads the hardware status register
+  (`APPLE_DVFS_STATUS`), so sampling it in 200 ms windows in which a
+  cluster's CPUs had no busy jiffies answers it. On this busy machine no
+  P cluster was idle for a whole 200 ms window in 30 s, so it needs a quiet
+  one. If the clock stays up, part of the "PD always" saving is leakage at a
+  voltage nobody needs, and lowering the request when a cluster goes idle
+  would add to it.
+
 * Whether iBoot arms the wake path (reconfig engine, boot CPU RVBAR) for a
   raw image as it does for a kernelcache.
 * The SMC/PMGR/PMP/AOP sleep handshake; macOS's sleep drain on this machine.
